(1 and 2) I think the question here really is how to handle the discrepancy if the online version potentially gets updated and you've bookmarked it, and stored it (Your  point one is harder for me to understand than two, but they sound sort of like the same sort of decision). You have a cached copy that's the thing you're using for position saving and for listening. So when you come back later, do you use that or the online version. I think the answer is to by default stick with the version at the time of bookmarking. Do not fetch anew if there's a stored version. However, within the listing of bookmarked articles, give a little reload button and then the user can intentionally reload. one of the main reasons I want to do this, partly to avoid extra network hits, but that's pretty minor. But I don't want to lose the position if the person has bookmarked it. It sort of also gives the user a way to pin a version of the article so that the application is able to just seamlessly maintain a play location. 
(3) Let's prompt "Resume from prior location?" with a yes/no. Yes, means the obvious start playing from where you were. Obviously, we may back up to the earliest beginning of the paragraph. That seems to be a sort of design limitation currently. Even if there is a no, if the user presses the play resume button in the control panel then it still resumes from that saved location. The intent is that audio starring by virtue of tapping on an article might be surprising (bad, avoid), but we do want to give them a couple of opportunities to be able to do that, both when re-opening (convenient) and via player controls (In case they want to scroll through and look at the article first, or just accidentially hit "no" on the first pop up)

